+# Pickup reminders
+
+For whoever changes how members are reminded of a pickup, or proposes sending the reminder by email to save the SMS
+cost. The day before a pickup, larder texts the member that their pantry order is ready. The reminder goes by SMS,
+not email, because the email trial of April to June 2025 doubled missed pickups; written when
+[issue 52](../../notes/issue-52.md) became the third request that year to switch.
+
+## Message
+
+`remind` in `larder/reminders.py` sends "Your pantry order is ready for pickup {pickup}." the day before the pickup
+slot. `CHANNEL` in the same file picks the channel; it is `"sms"`. The email branch remains from the trial.
+
+## Why SMS, not email
+
+SMS costs about 40 euros a month and email is free, so volunteers keep proposing to switch. From April to June 2025
+reminders went by email. The email provider reported that 34% of the reminder emails were never opened, and missed
+pickups rose from 6% of pantry orders to 13%. Each missed pickup is a packed bag whose fresh food is thrown away at
+closing time. Many members share one phone across the family and never check email, but they see a text. Reminders
+went back to SMS on July 1, 2025, and missed pickups were back to 6% by August.
+
+A proposal to switch to email needs to show how it would keep missed pickups at the SMS rate; the saving alone was
+weighed and lost.
